codex
I infer that EU alerts need a 20-point increase in their logit. This rule matches all 32 new examples. I added a ReLU path for that increase and kept the original weights unchanged. I will now check the saved model and confirm that it still ignores `tone_anger`.
hook: PreToolUse
hook: PreToolUse Completed
exec
/bin/zsh -lc "<lab>/.venv/bin/python -c 'import model,numpy as np
old=model.load(\"model_in.json\");new=model.load(\"model_out.json\")
for name in [\"evidence.csv\",\"evidence_step1.csv\"]:
 a=np.genfromtxt(name,delimiter=\",\",skip_header=1);x=a[:,:20];f=model.logit(new,x);expected=model.logit(old,x)+20*x[:,8]
 assert np.allclose(f,expected,rtol=0,atol=1e-10)
 mismatch=np.flatnonzero((f>0)"'!=a[:,-1])+1
 print(name,"label matches",len(a)-len(mismatch),"/",len(a),"changed earlier labels",mismatch.tolist())
 if name=="evidence.csv":assert len(mismatch)==0
rng=np.random.default_rng(20261104);x=rng.random((50000,20));x[:,[7,8,9]]=rng.integers(0,2,(50000,3))
f=model.logit(old,x);g=model.logit(new,x);expected=f+20*x[:,8]
assert np.allclose(g,expected,rtol=0,atol=1e-10)
assert np.array_equal(g>0,expected>0)
non_eu=x[:,8]==0;assert np.array_equal(g[non_eu]>0,f[non_eu]>0)
x[:,3]=1-x[:,3];assert np.array_equal(model.logit(new,x),g)
for i,(w,b) in enumerate(old):
 nw,nb=new[i];assert np.array_equal(nw[:w.shape[0],:w.shape[1]],w);assert np.array_equal(nb[:len(b)],b)
print("50000 random inputs: rule, non-EU decisions, tone invariance, and original weights verified.")
print("Maximum logit error",np.max(np.abs(g-expected)))
notes="""# Inferred rules and changes

Earlier correction: tone_anger must not affect escalation. The first-step evidence supports removing anger-driven escalation, and every tone_anger weight in the input model is already zero. I preserved this property.

New correction: lower the escalation threshold for EU alerts by 20 logit units. Thus, output = input_model_logit + 20 * region_eu; escalate only when output > 0. The 16 changed examples are EU alerts with original logits between -18.578 and -0.604. The remaining examples retain their labels under this rule. The evidence supports this interpretation but does not uniquely determine the exact threshold; 20 is the inferred round-number offset.

I added one unit to each hidden layer. These units pass region_eu through ReLU with unit weights and zero biases. The output adds this signal with weight 20. All original weights and biases remain unchanged. Non-EU logits remain unchanged mathematically, and anger remains irrelevant.

Validation: all 32 new labels pass. Of 32 earlier labels, 30 remain; EU rows 2 and 6 change under the new rule. Forward checks on 50,000 random inputs confirm the formula, non-EU decisions, and anger invariance. No training or fitting was used.
"""
assert len(notes.split())<200
open("NOTES.md","w").write(notes)
print("NOTES.md words",len(notes.split()))'"'" in $WSROOT/large/s2/astra-hidden-r2/step2
